<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Causal Inference Theory and Counterfactual Causal Inference</h2>

<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15361v1/causal_graph.svg" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/161;" width="429" height="161"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>(a) An example of a causal graph, where <math id="A1.F6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> transmits information to the outcome <math id="A1.F6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> via the mediator variable <math id="A1.F6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. (b) The process of counterfactual intervention, observing the influence of <math id="A1.F6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.F6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> via the mediator variable <math id="A1.F6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> by interfering the link between <math id="A1.F6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.F6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section first introduces the theory of causal inference, which serves as the cornerstone for multimodal causal mediation analysis in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Multimodal Causal Mediation Framework ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Beyond Spurious Signals: Debiasing Multimodal Large Language Models via Counterfactual Inference and Adaptive Expert Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Causal graphs are highly generalized analytical tools used to reveal causal dependencies between variables. The prediction process of a model can be defined as a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG) <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G={V,E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G={V,E}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the set of nodes <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> represents a series of intermediate factors involved in the prediction process, and the set of edges <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the causal effects between them. Figure <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix A Causal Inference Theory and Counterfactual Causal Inference ‣ Beyond Spurious Signals: Debiasing Multimodal Large Language Models via Counterfactual Inference and Adaptive Expert Routing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents an example of a causal graph with three variables. In this graph, uppercase letters denote random variables, and lowercase letters denote their actual observed values. The causal relationship from a reference variable <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> to an outcome variable <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> comprises two paths:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>: The direct effect path, representing the direct effect of <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to M\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to M\to Y</annotation></semantics></math>: The indirect effect path, representing the effect of <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> mediated by the intermediate variable <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Counterfactual inference involves applying counterfactual treatment conditions to the model and observing the outcome of the original input under these new conditions, thereby deducing the influence of the counterfactual factors. This process can be formally expressed. The outcome for the original input is:</p>
<table id="A1.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{x,m}=Y(X=x,M=m)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{x,m}=Y(X=x,M=m),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=M_{x}=M(X=x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=M_{x}=M(X=x)</annotation></semantics></math>. By applying a counterfactual treatment to <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to yield <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the counterfactual outcome:</p>
<table id="A1.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{x^{*},M_{x^{*}}}=Y(X=x^{*},M=M(X=x^{*}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{x^{*},M_{x^{*}}}=Y(X=x^{*},M=M(X=x^{*})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{x^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the value of the mediator <math id="A1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is changed to <math id="A1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, a scenario can be constructed where X is counterfactually set to <math id="A1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, but the mediator <math id="A1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> retains the value it would have taken under the original input <math id="A1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. The outcome in this case is <math id="A1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{x^{*},M_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{x^{*},M_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Causal effects quantify the difference between the outcome after intervening on a reference variable and the outcome in its natural (pre-intervention) state. According to causal theory, when the variable <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is changed from <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>, the Total Effect (TE) on the model’s outcome is defined as:</p>
<table id="A1.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TE=\text{DIFF}(Y_{x,M_{x}},Y_{x^{*},M_{x^{*}}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>DIFF</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TE=\text{DIFF}(Y_{x,M_{x}},Y_{x^{*},M_{x^{*}}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where DIFF represents a function that measures the difference between the predicted outcomes before and after the intervention. The Total Effect (TE) can be decomposed into the Natural Direct Effect (NDE) and the Total Indirect Effect (TIE). The NDE refers to the impact of a change in the causal variable <math id="A1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> (from <math id="A1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>) on the outcome <math id="A1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> when the mediator variable <math id="A1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is held at the level it would naturally assume if <math id="A1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> were counterfactually set:</p>
<table id="A1.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NDE=\text{DIFF}(Y_{x,M_{x^{*}}},Y_{x^{*},M_{x^{*}}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>DIFF</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NDE=\text{DIFF}(Y_{x,M_{x^{*}}},Y_{x^{*},M_{x^{*}}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">This aims to isolate the impact of the reference variable on the outcome variable, holding the mediator constant at <math id="A1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{x^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The Total Indirect Effect (TIE) represents the effect of the reference variable <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> indirectly influencing the outcome variable <math id="A1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> through the mediator <math id="A1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. It is calculated by subtracting NDE from TE:</p>
<table id="A1.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TIE=TE-NDE=\text{DIFF}(Y_{x,M_{x}},Y_{x,M_{x^{*}}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>DIFF</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TIE=TE-NDE=\text{DIFF}(Y_{x,M_{x}},Y_{x,M_{x^{*}}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, depending on the nature of the mediator <math id="A1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, NDE or TIE can be leveraged for unbiased estimation regarding the outcome variable Y:</p>
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the mediator <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is identified as a variable that potentially introduces bias and is not conducive to the final prediction. In this case, we hold <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> at the value <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{x^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{x^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and observe the unbiased effect of <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. This process is achieved through NDE.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the mediator <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> represents the pathway or features of interest, and only the effect mediated through <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is desired for an accurate prediction. Here, TIE can be used to calculate the indirect effect of <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding an unbiased estimate of this mediated effect.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we define M as the bias variable (representing spurious textual and visual context), and therefore, we employ NDE for unbiased estimation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Counterfactual Image Generation via Attention Masks</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algx1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
            
            
          MLLM <math id="algx1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="model" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">model</annotation></semantics></math>, image <math id="algx1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math>, prompt <math id="algx1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, number of model layers <math id="algx1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, image dimensions <math id="algx1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H,W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H,W</annotation></semantics></math>, image patch grid dimensions <math id="algx1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(patch_{h},patch_{w})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(patch_{h},patch_{w})</annotation></semantics></math>, enhancement factor <math id="algx1.l1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
            
            
          counterfactual image <math id="algx1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{cf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{cf}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
            
            
